<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Chad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> is a large, shallow lake in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, bordered today by Chad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Its basin was the heartland of the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>, whose economy depended on its resources and on trade routes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The lake region also linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> to wider networks of commerce and conflict.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-chad.jpg" alt="Lake Chad" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Chad_(MODIS_2018-06-07).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The federation covered about 4.7 million square kilometres, from the Atlantic to <b>Lake Chad</b> and from the Sahara to the Gulf of Guinea, with a population of well over ten million that approached twenty million by the late 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1257 an embassy from the king of Kanem, near <b>Lake Chad</b>, crossed the Sahara and presented the Hafsid ruler al-Mustansir with a giraffe, which caused a sensation in Tunis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Their towns became the leading markets between the middle Niger and <b>Lake Chad</b>, linking the trans-Saharan trade with the forest zone, and Hausa merchants established communities, called zongo in the south, from the Gold Coast to Cameroon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kanem–Bornu Empire originated around the ninth century north-east of <b>Lake Chad</b> as the Kingdom of Kanem, ruled first by the Duguwa dynasty, linked to the Zaghawa, and from the late eleventh century by the Sayfawa, who claimed descent from the legendary Yemeni hero Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lunsi tell of Tohazhie, the Red Hunter, who left Tunga east of <b>Lake Chad</b> with a small band of horsemen for Zamfara in present-day Nigeria and then Mali, where he married Pag Wabiga, daughter of the king, and fathered Kpoginumbo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…linked the caliphate to Tripoli and other North African termini across the desert, westward to the kola-producing forests of Asante, southward to the Yoruba markets, and eastward to Bornu and the <b>Lake Chad</b> basin, with cloth, leather goods, kola nuts and slaves among the principal commodities of exchange.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
